speechtext:
The House resumed, from June 17, consideration in committee of the following proposed resolution-Hon. Mr. Robb-Mr. Gordon in the chair:
That it is expedient to introduce a measure to provide :
(a)	that the Governor in Council may authorize advances to any one or more of the provincial governments for the purpose of assisting agriculture by enabling the provinces to make long term loans to farmers;
(b)	that such advances shall be secured by the issue and delivery to the Dominion of bonds of the provinces ;
(c)	that the aggregate of advances to be made by the Dominion shall not exceed ten million dollars ($10,000,000).

Hon. J. A. ROBB (Acting Minister of Finance) moved that the House go into committee to consider the following proposed resolution:
That it is expedient to bring in a measure respecting trade relations with Australia, and to provide,-
1.	That the Minister of Finance be authorized to enter into an agreement with the government of the Commonwealth of Australia under which Australia shall grant to goods the produce or manufacture of Canada when imported into Australia the benefits of the reduced rates of duty set out in schedule I to this resolution, and Canada may in return therefor grant to goods the produce or manufacture of Australia when imported direct into Canada the benefits of the rates of customs duty set out in schedule II to this resolution;
2. That after the said agreement is brought into force and so long as it remains in force, goods set out in schedule II to this resolution, the produce or manufacture of the Commonwealth of Australia, which are imported direct into Canada shall be admitted to Canada at the rates of duties set out in the said schedule;
3.	That the tariff concessions authorized by this resolution to be extended to goods the produce or manufacture of Australia shall be so extended on and after a day to be fixed by proclamation of the Governor in Council, which proclamation shall be published in the Canada Gazette;
4.	That subject to the provisions of The Customs Tariff, 1907, the Governor in Council may, by order in council, extend the said advantages to goods the produce or manufacture of any British country;
5.	That the Governor in Council may make such orders and regulations as are deemed necessary to carry out the provisions and intent of this resolution and of the said agreement and may, upon giving six months' notice to the government of the Commonwealth of Australia of his intention so to do, order and direct that the tariff concessions extended to Australia by this resolution, shall cease and determine, whereupon they shall cease and determine accordingly;
6.	That the operation of all laws inconsistent with the giving to the provisions of the said agreement and of this resolution their full effect shall from time to time be suspended to the extent of such inconsistency.
Schedule I
(A) British preferential tariff concessions by Australia to Canada:
Australia will grant to the undermentioned goods, the produce or manufacture of Canada, when imported into Australia, the rates of duty for the time being applicable to goods to which the British preferential tariff in the customs tariff 1921-24 applies, viz:
Australian Treaty

I should like to make a
remark or two, but I do not know whether this is the proper place to mention the matter. There has been a great deal of complaint that grades in the eastern division were not of the same standard as those in the western division, and there is some complaint that the survey board in the eastern division should be appointed from persons nominated by the boards of trade of Toronto and Montreal. I do not wish to delay the committee, and I am not going to make any protest against this section, but I would like to present a resolution passed by a local of the United Farmers' Association at Bawlf, Alberta. It reads as follows:
Whereas the grading of Canadian grain is dependent on the judgment of deputy inspectors;
And whereas, operators of private elevators (mixing houses) by reason of direct contact with inspectors may influence them to pass questionable mixtures;
And whereas several large Canadian exporters, millers and corporations operate public elevators in the eastern divisions, and under section 10 of "Rules and Regulations for Sample Market Trading," notwithstanding the provisions of the Canada Grain Act (sec-

tion 229, subsection 5) to the contrary, are permitted to mix grains for their patrons and possibly operate to their own advantage, which practices should be carefully and impartially supervised;
And whereas we believe the grading of "Country run" grain and "Export" grain should be more uniformly the same as to quality;
And whereas we believe uniformity of grading can best be attained bj' frequent interchanging of deputy
inspectors;
Therefore be it resolved that we recommend to our representatives at Ottawa that in the revision of the Canada Grain Act, provision be made for the systematic interchanging of deputy inspectors, particularly as to staff inspecting grains into terminal elevators, and other deputy inspectors also, as to the Eastern and Western routes.
I want to suggest that while we have the chairman of the Board of Grain Commissioners here, if the survey board in the eastern division are to be nominated by the boards of trade of Toronto and Montreal, many of whom are in this grain trade and it is presumed they would interest themselves in the appointment of this survey board, I think it would be in the interests of the grain producers and of the whole grain trade that these inspectors should be changed occasionally from the eastern to the western inspection division, and in that way possibly they would arrive at some uniformity in the grading of the wheat in those two divisions. At the present time there is a feeling that the grades in the eastern division are lower in standard than the grades in the western division, and as I have pointed out it is affected by this section, because the survey board is the final court of appeal for the grading of our grain. I think that the best way to keep the grading uniform between the two divisions would be by taking the western inspectors and having them work in the eastern division for certain periods of the year, and placing the eastern inspectors in the western division. It is possible this may be covered under some other section, and I may be making the statement at the wrong time, but I do not know on what other section of the act I could bring it up.

Certainly, I understand it; the pools own the grain all right. The member for Qu'Appelle (Mi. Millar) says that the act should read in this way:
A private elevator shall receive only such grain as is the property of the person or corporation operating such elevator, and no such elevator shall conduct a public storage business or receive any grain upon terms requiring another person to pay storage charges thereon or in respect thereof: Provided, however, that it shall be lawful for the organizations of grain producers known as grain pools and incorporated in the provinces of Manitoba, Saskatchewan and Alberta by acts of the respective legislatures of such provinces, and for any other person or corporation which in the opinion of the board, is empowered to act and is in fact acting on behalf of or in co-operation with them or any of them, to operate private elevators and to receive into such elevators grain shipped by such producers as are members of any one of the said grain pools;
Now then, if that goes through no man who is not in the grain pool can have the advantage of selling his grain through the owner of a private elevator and obtaining the premium that the private elevator will pay because of its mixing privilege.
